**Singleton.** Guarantee one instance with a global access point. A thread-safe, lazy option is the initialisation-on-demand holder, or an enum.

    public class Config {
        private Config() {}
        private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }
        public static Config getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
    }

Alternatives: eager static field; double-checked locking requires a `volatile` field; `enum Config { INSTANCE }` is also safe against reflection and serialization. Drawbacks: hidden global state, hard to test, tight coupling. Prefer dependency injection when possible.

**Factory Method.** Define an interface for creating an object and let the creator decide which concrete class to instantiate, so client code depends only on the abstraction.

    interface Notification { void send(String msg); }
    class Email implements Notification { public void send(String m) {} }
    class Sms implements Notification { public void send(String m) {} }

    class NotificationFactory {
        static Notification create(String type) {
            return switch (type) {
                case "email" -> new Email();
                case "sms" -> new Sms();
                default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
            };
        }
    }

This is a simple factory; the classic Factory Method makes the creator an abstract class with an overridable `create()`. To make it scalable (open for extension), keep a registry `Map<String, Supplier<Notification>>` so new types register without editing the factory (Open/Closed Principle).

**Observer.** A subject keeps a list of observers and notifies them when its state changes; observers subscribe and unsubscribe. This gives loose coupling (one-to-many). Example: a price or stock feed where a `PriceTicker` notifies `Dashboard`, `AlertService` and `Logger`. Java: `interface Observer { void update(Event e); }`, and the subject has `register`, `unregister` and `notifyAll`. Issues to mention: notification order, exceptions in one observer, memory leaks from forgotten subscriptions, and thread safety (use `CopyOnWriteArrayList`). Real examples: event listeners, publish/subscribe messaging.
